    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. Could you go into a bit more detail about the specific step where the enzyme is blocked? Like, what is physically happening at the molecular level to prevent that conversion from taking place?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "At the molecular level, Lisinopril functions as a competitive inhibitor of the ACE enzyme. It binds to the active site of ACE, physically occupying the pocket where Angiotensin I would normally dock. This binding prevents the catalytic domain of the enzyme from processing Angiotensin I, thus stopping the peptidyl chain cleavage necessary to form Angiotensin II. Essentially, Lisinopril acts as a molecular plug, rendering the ACE enzyme temporarily inactive.",
      "source": "target",
